  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Simplify $\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\alpha }{{tan}^{2}\u2061\\alpha +\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\alpha +1}.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}\u2061\\alpha ={sec}^{2}\u2061\\alpha  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}\u2061\\alpha ={sec}^{2}\u2061\\alpha -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}\u2061\\alpha -{tan}^{2}\u2061\\alpha =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\alpha }{{tan}^{2}\u2061\\alpha +\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\alpha +1} & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\alpha }{{sec}^{2}\u2061\\alpha -1+\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\alpha +1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\alpha }{{sec}^{2}\u2061\\alpha +\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\alpha }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can further simplify our expression by factoring it out, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\alpha }{{sec}^{2}\u2061\\alpha +\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\alpha } & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\alpha }{\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\alpha (\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\alpha +1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{1+\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\alpha }}{\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\alpha \\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\alpha +1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\alpha } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\alpha \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\alpha }{{tan}^{2}\u2061\\alpha +\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\alpha +1}=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\alpha .\n$$",
